#   ‹open›      path -> fd         open for reading (O_RDONLY)
#   ‹create›    path -> fd         open for writing: create, truncate, mode 0644
#   ‹close›     fd ->
#   ‹fread›     fd n -> list       ONE read(2) of up to n bytes; empty = EOF
#   ‹fwrite›    list fd ->         write every element's low byte, looping over
#                                  short writes
#   ‹readfile›  path -> list       the whole file
#   ‹writefile› list path ->       create/truncate path, write list, close
# Failure: ‹throw› with `cannot <verb> <path> (<ENAME>)` — verb open, create,
# read, write or close; the fd words name `fd N` instead of a path; an errno
# without a name reads `errno N`.  The positive errno is stored first in the
# 1B errno cell 5179712 (0x4F0940, REGISTRY §3.1); it is never cleared.
# State (1B slot, 8-byte cells): 5179720 errno name list · 5179728 the subject
# path (0 = an fd word) · 5179736 the subject fd · 5179744 a path buffer, then
# ‹_1b_dec›'s n · 5179752 / 5179760 ‹_1b_dec›'s buffer and cursor.  No word
# here calls user code, so one global set of cells is enough.  Looping words
# frame with X/Y and never hold s5 (A's) across a call.  Names ‹_1b…› are the
# helpers.  Results are tagged with `1 48\shl|` like every prelude list (1A).
# ‹_1b_tobytes›  list -> buf   a raw A block holding each element's low byte,
